    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the test passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, BUILD SUCCESS). The AUDIT line was emitted: `AUDIT - Owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-11`.\n\n## What I did\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (one small function class per step, wired in YAML), I added an audit step to the create-owner pipeline:\n\n**New function class** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`:\n```java\npublic class AuditOwnerCreated {\n    private static final Logger audit = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");\n\n    public void service(@Val Owner owner) {\n        audit.info(\"Owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n                owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 `src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`: inserted an `audit` step between `save` and `respond`, also under `govern: [ transaction ]`:\n```yaml\nsave:\n  class: ...SaveOwner\n  next: audit\naudit:\n  class: ...AuditOwnerCreated\n  govern: [ transaction ]\n  next: respond\n```\n\n### Why it's placed there\nThe step runs **after `save`**, so the DB-generated `owner.getId()` is populated (the same object is read back via `@Val`, per the `Out`/`@Val` contract). The logger is named `AUDIT` \u2014 exactly what the test's `AuditLogCapture` attaches its appender to \u2014 and the line carries the owner id, the assigned `customerCode`, and the `registrationDate` (logged as ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`, matching the GET response text the test compares against).\n\nThe existing test `AcceptanceTest.coreLogsAuditOnCreate` now passes.",
